            <section class="mt-content-container">
                <div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_1"><span id="A._Radical_Formation"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">A. Radical Formation</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">The reactions of the glycosyl chlorides <strong>23</strong> and <strong>24</strong> provide examples of stereoselectivity in pyranos-1-yl radical formation that depend on orbital interactions (Schemes 9 and 10).<sup>25,26</sup> The difference in their reaction rates is linked to the orientation of the C<sub>2</sub>&ndash;O bond in each of these ster&shy;eo&shy;isomers. The <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D</span>-mannopyranosyl chloride <strong>23</strong> reacts with tri-<em>n</em>-butyltin hydride 7.8 times more rapidly than does the epimeric <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D</span>-gluco&shy;pyran&shy;osyl chloride <strong>24</strong>. For <strong>23</strong> the C-2 acetoxy group has an orientation that allows transition-state stabil&shy;i&shy;zation by the orbital interactions shown in Scheme 9. These are the same interactions associated with the quasi-anomeric effect. (The quasi-ano&shy;meric effect, discussed in <a title="V. Quasi-Anomeric Radical Stabilization" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I%3A_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/06%3A_Radical_Structure/V._Quasi-Anomeric_Radical_Stabilization" rel="internal">Section V</a> of Chapter 6, provides an explanation for the con&shy;formations adopted by pyranos-1-yl radicals.<sup>27,28</sup>) Since these orbital interactions are developing at the transition state, they should be responsible, at least in part, for the greater reactivity of <strong>23</strong> when compared to <strong>24</strong>. Such stabilization is minimal for reaction of <strong>24</strong> because the &sigma;* orbital associated with the C<sub>2</sub>&ndash;O bond does not have the proper, transition-state orientation to assist significantly in stabil&shy;ization (Scheme 10).</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s9.png" class="default internal" style="width: 395px; height: 387px;" width="395px" height="387px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44042/s9.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=395&amp;height=387" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s10.png" class="default internal" style="width: 450px; height: 421px;" width="450px" height="421px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44043/s10.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=450&amp;height=421" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">A critical assumption about the reactions shown in Schemes 9 and 10 is that the first step (chlorine-atom abstraction by the tri-<em>n</em>-butyltin radical), rather than the second step (hydrogen-atom abstraction from tri-<em>n</em>-butyltin hydride by the carbohydrate radical) is rate-determining. Such an assump&shy;tion is reason&shy;able because chlorine-atom abstraction in free-radical dehalogenation of alkyl chlor&shy;ides is known to be rate-determining,<sup>29</sup> but it is not a certainty because for reac&shy;tion of iodides, bro&shy;mides, and some very reactive chlorides, hydrogen-atom abstraction from tri-<em>n</em>-butyltin hydride is the rate-determining step.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_2"><span id="B._Radical_Reaction"></span><h2 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">B. Radical Reaction</h2>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_3"><span id="1._The_Role_of_Radical_Conformation"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">1. The Role of Radical Conformation</h3>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_4"><span id="a._The_Curtin-Hammett_Principle"></span><h4 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">a. The Curtin-Hammett Principle</h4>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">Consider conformations X and Y of a radical that is undergoing an addition reaction to give the products P<sub>x</sub> and P<sub>y</sub> (Scheme 11). One possibility is that interconversion of X and Y is rapid com&shy;pared to their reactions. In this situation the Curtin&ndash;Hammett principle applies; that is, the ratio of the products depends only on the relative energies of the transition states leading to their for&shy;mation. (A more detailed statement of the Curtin-Hammet prin&shy;ciple is &ldquo;the relative amounts of products formed from two pertinent conformers are completely independent of the relative pop&shy;u&shy;la&shy;tions of the conformers and depend only on the difference in free energy of the transition states, provided the rates of reaction are slower than the rates of conform&shy;ational inter&shy;con&shy;version&rdquo;.<sup>30</sup>) An energy diagram showing a situation in which the Curtin-Hammet principle applies is found in Fig&shy;ure 2. If in this reaction P<sub>x</sub> and P<sub>y</sub> are stereoisomers, the overall process will form P<sub>y</sub> stereo&shy;se&shy;lec&shy;tively, even though conformer X is present in greater amount.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s11.png" class="default internal" style="width: 340px; height: 108px;" width="340px" height="108px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44044/s11.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=340&amp;height=108" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="f2.png" class="default internal" style="width: 430px; height: 329px;" width="430px" height="329px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44045/f2.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=430&amp;height=329" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">A different situation exists for the reaction shown in Figure 3. In this case the interconversion of radicals X and Y is slow compared to their reac&shy;tions. Under these conditions (the Curtin&ndash;Hammett principle not in effect) the conformation present in greatest amount determines the ster&shy;eo&shy;selectivity of the reaction; thus, if X is the major conformer, P<sub>x</sub> will be formed prefer&shy;ent&shy;i&shy;ally.&shy;</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="f3.png" class="default internal" style="width: 415px; height: 319px;" width="415px" height="319px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44046/f3.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=415&amp;height=319" /></p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_5"><span id="b._An_Initial_Proposal"></span><h4 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">b. An Initial Proposal</h4>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">The explanation for the stereoselectivity of the reactions of pyranos-1-yl radicals has changed over time as new information about radical structure has become available. The first proposal for the stereo&shy;selective for&shy;mation of the reduction products <strong>30</strong> and <strong>31</strong> from reaction of the <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D</span>&#8209;gluco&shy;pyran&shy;osyl bromide <strong>27</strong> with tri-<em>n</em>-butyltin deuteride was that product yields reflected the relative amounts of <strong>28</strong> and <strong>29</strong> present in the reaction mixture (Scheme 12).<sup>31</sup> (Inherent in this proposal was the assumption that <strong>28</strong> and <strong>29</strong> were the most stable structures for this pyranos-1-yl radical and that they reacted more rapidly than they equilibrated.) The major component in this proposed pseudoequilibrium was thought to be <strong>28</strong> because this radical would be stabilized more effec&shy;tively than <strong>29</strong> by inter&shy;action of the or&shy;bital centered on C-1 with the <em>p&#8209;</em>type orbital on the ring oxygen atom (Scheme 12). This interpretation subse&shy;quently had to be revised because ESR spectral analysis showed that neither <strong>28</strong> nor <strong>29</strong> was as stable as the distorted B<sub>2,5</sub> boat con&shy;for&shy;mation <strong>26</strong> (Scheme 13).<sup>27,28</sup></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s12.png" class="default internal" style="width: 375px; height: 452px;" width="375px" height="452px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44047/s12.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=375&amp;height=452" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s13.png" class="default internal" style="width: 445px; height: 154px;" width="445px" height="154px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44048/s13.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=445&amp;height=154" /></p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_6"><span id="c._A_Revised_Explanation"></span><h4 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">c. A Revised Explanation</h4>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_7"><span id="(1)._Steric_Effects"></span><h5 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">(1). Steric Effects</h5>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">One possible explanation for stereoselectivity in reactions involv&shy;ing the <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D</span>-glucopyranos-1-yl radical <strong>26</strong> is that steric effects are determining this selec&shy;tivity just as they do in the reactions of many other carbohydrate radi&shy;cals. For this explanation to be correct, approach to C-1 by Bu<sub>3</sub>SnD from the &alpha; face of <strong>26</strong> would have to be less hindered than a similar approach to its &beta; face. With the neighboring 2-<em>0</em>-acetyl group shielding &alpha;-face reaction at C-1 (Scheme 13), it is difficult to see how the least hindered pathway to C-1 would involve approach to the &alpha; face of the radical.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">Another view of the difficulty with steric interactions being the control&shy;ling factor in stereo&shy;se&shy;lec&shy;tivity of the reactions of <strong>26</strong> comes from comparing these reactions with those of the pyran&shy;os-4-yl radical <strong>7</strong> and the pyranos-3-yl radical <strong>8</strong> (<a title="t3.png" rel="internal" class="iconitext-16 ext-png" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/43979/t3.png?revision=1">Table 3</a>). For neither <strong>7</strong> nor <strong>8</strong> is the stereo&shy;selec&shy;tiv&shy;ity of reaction with Bu<sub>3</sub>SnD large, but for each the major stereoisomer comes from reaction on the face of the radical opposite to that containing the shielding groups on the adjacent carbon atoms. This result stands in contrast to the reaction of the pyranos-1-yl radical <strong>26</strong>, where the ster&shy;e&shy;oselectivity is not only much larger, but Bu<sub>3</sub>SnD approaches this radical from the face containing the only shielding group on a neigh&shy;boring carbon atom. The &alpha;&#8209;face stereoselectivity of <strong>26</strong> is not limited to reaction with Bu<sub>3</sub>SnD. Addi&shy;tion of this radical to acrylonitrile also is stereoselectively from the more hindered &alpha; face (eq 4).</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="(4).png" class="default internal" style="width: 440px; height: 145px;" width="440px" height="145px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44049/(4).png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=440&amp;height=145" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">The <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D</span>-mannopyranos-1-yl radical <strong>25</strong> exhibits even greater &alpha;-face selectivity in deuterium ab&shy;straction (Scheme 14) and addition to acrylonitrile (eq 5) than does its <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D</span>-gluco epimer <strong>26</strong> (<a title="s13.png" rel="internal" class="iconitext-16 ext-png" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44048/s13.png?revision=1">Scheme 13</a>, eq 4). While steric effects could explain the stereoselectivity in the reactions of <strong>25</strong>, they are unable to rationalize the selectivity in the reactions of both <strong>25</strong> and <strong>26</strong>; however, for each of these radicals stereoselectivity does have a link to radical confor&shy;mation.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s14.png" class="default internal" style="width: 340px; height: 312px;" width="340px" height="312px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44050/s14.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=340&amp;height=312" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="(5).png" class="default internal" style="width: 435px; height: 139px;" width="435px" height="139px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44051/(5).png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=435&amp;height=139" /></p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_8"><span id="(2)._Interconversion_of_Conformations"></span><h5 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">(2). Interconversion of Conformations</h5>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">For the <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D</span>&#8209;mannopyranos-1-yl (<strong>25</strong>) and <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D</span>&#8209;lyxopyranos-1-yl (<strong>35</strong>) radicals only the <sup>4</sup>C<sub>1</sub> chair conformation can be detected in the ESR spectrum of each (Scheme 15).<sup>27,28,32</sup> Struc&shy;tur&shy;ally these radicals differ only at C-5 where the equatorial CH<sub>2</sub>OAc substituent in <strong>25</strong> &shy;is replaced by a hydrogen atom in <strong>35</strong>. Even though the structures <strong>25</strong> and <strong>35</strong> are quite similar in the vicinity of the radical center, the stereoselectivity of their reactions is quite different. The radical <strong>25</strong> adds stereoselectively to acrylo&shy;nitrile to give only the product arising from reaction at its &alpha; face, but the radical <strong>35</strong> reacts from both its &alpha; and &beta; faces (Scheme 15).<sup>32</sup></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s15.png" class="default internal" style="width: 415px; height: 487px;" width="415px" height="487px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44052/s15.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=415&amp;height=487" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">Since an equa&shy;torial substituent at C-5 is remote from the reacting center at C-1, steric effects alone cannot explain the differ&shy;ence in reac&shy;tivity between these two (<strong>25</strong> and <strong>35</strong>). Findings concerning the reactivity of <strong>25</strong> and <strong>35</strong> (Scheme 15) are echoed in the reac&shy;tions of the <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D</span>-glucopyranos-1-yl radical <strong>26</strong> and the <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D&#8209;</span>xylopyranos-1-yl radical <strong>36</strong> (Scheme 16), further; observable conformations and reactivity <strong>26</strong> and <strong>36</strong> point to a possible explanation for the difference in stereoselectivity in the reactions of this pair (<strong>26</strong> and <strong>36</strong>) as well as the difference in stereoselectivity in the reactions of <strong>25</strong> and <strong>35</strong>.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s16.png" class="default internal" style="width: 420px; height: 495px;" width="420px" height="495px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44053/s16.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=420&amp;height=495" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">The ESR spectrum of <strong>26</strong> shows it to exist in a distorted B<sub>2,5</sub> boat confor&shy;mation, but for <strong>36</strong> B<sub>2,5</sub> boat, <sup>1,4</sup>B boat, and <sup>1</sup>C<sub>4</sub> chair con&shy;for&shy;mations also can be detected.<sup>6</sup> Conformational population, there&shy;fore, changes considerable when the CH<sub>2</sub>OAc substituent at C-5 in <strong>26</strong> is replaced by a hydrogen atom. This finding raises the possibility that the difference in stereo&shy;selec&shy;tivity in the reac&shy;tions of these radicals many be related to population and reactivity of confor&shy;mational isomers (Scheme 16).</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_9"><span id="(3)._Conformational_Mobility"></span><h5 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">(3). Conformational Mobility</h5>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">If B<sub>2,5</sub> con&shy;for&shy;mations react from their &alpha; face, and <sup>1,4</sup>B or <sup>4</sup>C<sub>1</sub> conformers (or both) from their &beta; face (<a title="s16.png" rel="internal" class="iconitext-16 ext-png" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44053/s16.png?revision=1">Scheme 16</a>), conformational population and mobility could explain the observed stereo&shy;selec&shy;tivity in the reactions of radicals <strong>26</strong> and <strong>36</strong>.<sup>6</sup> According to this explan&shy;ation, interconversion among conformers of the radical <strong>36</strong> would need to be more rapid than reactions of these radicals (Curtin-Hammett principle in effect). Since the assumption is that <sup>1,4</sup>B boat or <sup>1</sup>C<sub>4</sub> con&shy;for&shy;mers give &beta;-face reaction products,<sup>6</sup> the conclu&shy;sion is that one or both of these conformers is much more reactive than the B<sub>2,5</sub> conformation and that this very reactive confor&shy;mation does so in a highly stereo&shy;selective fashion. A further part of this argu&shy;ment is that the CH<sub>2</sub>OAc substituent attached to C-5 in the radical <strong>26</strong> stabilizes the B<sub>2,5 </sub>con&shy;formation and, in so doing, increases its population to the point that little reaction occurs from other conformers (Scheme 16).</p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">A similar explanation exists for the difference in stereoselectivity between radicals <strong>25</strong> and <strong>35</strong> (<a title="s15.png" rel="internal" class="iconitext-16 ext-png" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44052/s15.png?revision=1">Scheme 15</a>). If interconversion among the con&shy;for&shy;mers of the radical <strong>35</strong> is faster than reaction with acrylonitrile, it is pos&shy;sible to form a &beta;-<em>C</em>-glycoside by reaction with a minor conformer. For the radical <strong>25</strong> the equatorial CH<sub>2</sub>OAc substituent at C-5 must stabilize the <sup>4</sup>C<sub>1</sub> con&shy;for&shy;mation suffici&shy;ently that the concentration of minor conformers is too small for them to account for significant product formation.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_10"><span id="(4)._Transition-State_Stabilization:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect"></span><h5 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">(4). Transition-State Stabilization: The Kinetic Anomeric Effect</h5>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">To understand how radical conformation can have such a pronounced effect on stereo&shy;selec&shy;tivity in the reactions of pyranos-1-yl radicals, it is instructive to examine transition-state sta&shy;bil&shy;i&shy;zation. The major stereoisomer formed in these reactions results from a radical adopt&shy;ing a con&shy;for&shy;mation that allows the stabilizing interaction between the <em>p</em>-type orbit&shy;als on C-1 and the ring oxygen atom to be maintained to the greatest extent in the transition-state. This confor&shy;ma&shy;tion-de&shy;pendent, stereo&shy;elec&shy;tronic, transition-state sta&shy;bil&shy;i&shy;za&shy;tion is referred to as the kine&shy;tic<sup>33,34</sup> or radical<sup>35,36</sup> anomeric effect. (We will use &ldquo;kinetic anomeric effect&rdquo; in des&shy;cribing this phenom&shy;enon.)</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s17.png" class="default internal" style="width: 435px; height: 625px;" width="435px" height="625px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44054/s17.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=435&amp;height=625" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">The kinetic anomeric effect offers a rationale for the <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D&#8209;</span>mannopyranos-1&#8209;yl radical <strong>25</strong> adding to acrylonitrile exclusively from the &alpha; face of the pyranoid ring (<a title="s15.png" rel="internal" class="iconitext-16 ext-png" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44052/s15.png?revision=1">Scheme 15</a>). Only for &alpha;-face reac&shy;tion will stabilizing inter&shy;action between the <em>p</em>-type orbitals on the ring oxygen atom and the singly occu&shy;pied orbital on C-1 be maintained in the transition state (Scheme 17). Predicting stereo&shy;se&shy;lec&shy;tivity in the reaction of a conformation&shy;ally mobile radical can be a challenging task because more than one conformation is accessible and determining which conformation is the most reactive may be dif&shy;fi&shy;cult. This means that minor conformers, even ones that cannot be detected by ESR spec&shy;tros&shy;copy, can play a major role in determining reaction stereoselectivity. For the <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D</span>-gluco&shy;pyran&shy;os-1-yl radical <strong>26</strong> the only con&shy;for&shy;mation detectable by ESR spectroscopy is a distorted B<sub>2,5</sub> boat,<sup>27,28</sup> but <sup>1,4</sup>B and <sup>1</sup>C<sub>4</sub> conformations may be only modestly higher in energy and, therefore, easily access&shy;ible. For the B<sub>2,5</sub> conformation, reaction from the &alpha;-face of the pyranoid ring main&shy;tains stabilizing orbital interaction more effectively than reaction from its &beta; face (Scheme 18).</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s18.png" class="default internal" style="width: 440px; height: 505px;" width="440px" height="505px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44055/s18.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=440&amp;height=505" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">Reac&shy;tion does occur, however, to a small extent (5%) from the &beta; face of the pyranoid ring in the radical <strong>26</strong>. The &beta; anomer formed as a minor product could come from an accessible but unde&shy;tected conformation, such as the <sup>1</sup>C<sub>4</sub>. Maintaining orbital inter&shy;actions in a <sup>1</sup>C<sub>4</sub> chair conformation would favor the &beta;-face addition that leads to the minor product (Scheme 19). If this is the way in which &beta;-face addition takes place, then stereoselectivity depends not only on which conformations are acces&shy;sible and highly reactive but also on which face of a given conformer maintains the reac&shy;tion-pro&shy;moting orbital inter&shy;action that stabilizes the transition state.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s19.png" class="default internal" style="width: 425px; height: 643px;" width="425px" height="643px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44056/s19.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=425&amp;height=643" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">Another explanation for the formation of the minor product in the reac&shy;tion of the radical <strong>26</strong> is that this product results from a small amount of &beta;&#8209;face addition to the radical in its B<sub>2,5</sub> con&shy;for&shy;ma&shy;tion. Such an explanation, however, fails to explain greater &beta;-face addition for the radical <strong>36</strong>, when compared to <strong>26</strong> (<a title="s16.png" rel="internal" class="iconitext-16 ext-png" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44053/s16.png?revision=1">Scheme 16</a>), and is incompatible with the information, discussed in the next section, on reactions of radicals with restricted confor&shy;mations.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">Although the case is strong for radical conformation playing a critical role in stereo&shy;selec&shy;tiv&shy;ity of reactions of pyranos-1-yl radicals, uncertainty remains about how to identify the reactive con&shy;formation when several may be present and undergoing reaction. This uncertainty is effect&shy;tively eliminated where radicals with restricted conformations are concerned. Study of such radi&shy;cals has provided considerable insight into the power of the kinetic anomeric effect in determining reaction stereo&shy;selec&shy;tivity.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_11"><span id="(5)._Restricted_Conformations"></span><h5 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">(5). Restricted Conformations</h5>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">A restricted conformation for a radical is one that is highly favored over others due to some struc&shy;tural feature, such as an additional ring system. The radi&shy;cals <strong>37</strong> (Scheme 20) and <strong>47</strong> (Scheme 21) have pyranoid rings restricted to <sup>4</sup>C<sub>1</sub> and <sup>1</sup>C<sub>4</sub> chair conformations, respectively. This restriction is caused by the pres&shy;ence of appropriately placed, additional rings.<sup>33</sup> A second ring restricts con&shy;for&shy;mational change in the radical <strong>37</strong> by creating a <em>trans</em>-decalin-type structure. For the radical <strong>47</strong> a bridge produces a bicyclic structure that creates a rigid, conformationally restricted system.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s20.png" class="default internal" style="width: 435px; height: 691px;" width="435px" height="691px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44057/s20.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=435&amp;height=691" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s21.png" class="default internal" style="width: 345px; height: 675px;" width="345px" height="675px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44058/s21.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=345&amp;height=675" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">(a). Explanation for Reaction Stereoselectivity</p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">For the radical <strong>47</strong> maintaining stabilizing orbital inter&shy;action in the transition state requires approach of a reacting molecule, such as tri-<em>n</em>-butyltin deuteride, to the &beta;-face of the pyranoid ring (Scheme 21). This means that reaction proceeding according to the kinetic anomeric effect will give a product (<strong>48</strong>) with an axial deuterium atom at C-1. The high stereoselectivity of this reaction (<strong>48/49</strong> = 99/1) sup&shy;ports the idea that conformational and stereoelectronic effects together have a powerful influence on the reactions of pyranos-1-yl radicals. Such an idea is reinforced in a dra&shy;ma&shy;tic fashion by the reaction of the radical <strong>37 </strong>(Scheme 20). In this case in order to benefit from kinetic anomeric stabilization, the deuterium donor must approach the radical from the &alpha; face of the pyranoid ring. &alpha;&#8209;Face selectivity (<strong>39</strong>/<strong>40</strong> = 97/3) in this reaction (the pyranoid ring restricted to a <sup>4</sup>C<sub>1</sub> conformation) is nearly as great as the &beta;&#8209;face selectivity (<strong>48</strong>/<strong>49</strong>= 99/1) in the reaction of <strong>47</strong> (pyranoid ring restricted to a <sup>1</sup>C<sub>4</sub> conformation). Changing the radical con&shy;for&shy;mation then com&shy;pletely changes reaction stereoselectivity;<sup>33</sup> furthermore, the selectivity observed in each case is that predicted by the kinetic anomeric effect.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">When Bu<sub>3</sub>SnCH<sub>2</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub> replaces Bu<sub>3</sub>SnD as the molecule reacting with the radical <strong>37</strong>, the stereoselectivity decreases somewhat, although it remains high.<sup>33</sup> [The ratio of &alpha;-face to &beta;-face reac&shy;tion decreases from 97/3 (<strong>39</strong>/<strong>40</strong>) to 91/9 (<strong>41</strong>/<strong>42</strong>) (Scheme 20).] This modest decrease in stereo&shy;selec&shy;tiv&shy;ity disappears when the hydroxy group at C-2 is replaced by a hydrogen atom: that is, the stereo&shy;selectivity for the reaction of the 2-deoxy radical <strong>38</strong> with Bu<sub>3</sub>SnD is essent&shy;ially the same as that for reaction with Bu<sub>3</sub>SnCH<sub>2</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub> (Scheme 20). Steric hindrance associated with the C-2 hydroxy group, there&shy;fore, may be respon&shy;sible for the small difference in stereoselectivity observed for reac&shy;tions of the radical <strong>37</strong> with Bu<sub>3</sub>SnD and Bu<sub>3</sub>SnCH<sub>2</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub>. The overall picture, however, remains one in which steric effects have, at most, a minor role in deter&shy;mining ster&shy;e&shy;o&shy;se&shy;lec&shy;tivity in the reactions of these two radi&shy;cals (<strong>37</strong> and <strong>38</strong>).</p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">(b). &sigma;*-Orbital Interaction</p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">The &sigma;* orbital of the C<sub>2</sub>&ndash;O bond in a pyranos-1-yl radical contributes to the stability of these radicals by interacting with the <em>p</em>-type orbitals on C-1 and the ring oxygen atom. Radical sta&shy;bil&shy;i&shy;zation of this type plays a critical role in determining preferred radical conformation (see <a title="http://chemwiki.ucdavis.edu/Organic_Chemistry/Carbohydrates/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates/I%3A_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate__Radicals/06%3A_Radical_Structure/IV._Radical_Conformation#d._The_Quasi-Anomeric_Effect" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I%3A_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/06%3A_Radical_Structure/IV._Radical_Conformation#d._The_Quasi-Anomeric_Effect" rel="internal">Section IV.A.2.d.</a> of Chapter 6). A question raised by these orbital interactions concerns whether this type of stabilization also affects stereoselectivity in reactions of pyranos-1-yl radicals. The more rapid reaction of the <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D</span>-mannopyranosyl chloride <strong>23</strong> (<a title="s9.png" rel="internal" class="iconitext-16 ext-png" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44042/s9.png?revision=1">Scheme 9</a>) when compared to the epimeric <span style="font-size:9.0pt;">D</span>&#8209;glu&shy;co&shy;pyranosyl chloride <strong>24</strong> (Scheme 10) indicates that it may. Even for <strong>24</strong>, however, &sigma;*-orbital inter&shy;action is not essential because reaction still takes place (although less rapidly) even with minimal participation from this orbital (Scheme 10). Study of radicals with restricted conformations helps to clarify the role of the C<sub>2</sub>&ndash;O &sigma;* orbital in stereoselectivity of reac&shy;tions of pyranos-1-yl radi&shy;cals.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">Because the pyranoid rings in radicals <strong>37</strong> and <strong>38</strong> (<a title="s20.png" rel="internal" class="iconitext-16 ext-png" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44057/s20.png?revision=1">Scheme 20</a>) are restricted to <sup>4</sup>C<sub>1</sub> con&shy;for&shy;ma&shy;tions by their <em>trans</em>-decalin-like ring systems, the reactions of these radicals provide insight into the effect of a C<sub>2</sub>&ndash;O bond (in particular, its &sigma;*-orbital) on the stereoselectivity of the reactions of pyranos-1-yl radicals. The radical <strong>37</strong>, for example, reacts in a highly stereoselective fashion with tri-<em>n</em>-butyltin deuteride even though the &sigma;* orbital of the C<sub>2</sub>&ndash;O bond is not aligned to assist in transition-state stabilization (Figure 4). The radical <strong>38</strong> completely lacks a C-2 substituent; yet, it also undergoes highly stereo&shy;se&shy;lec&shy;tive reaction (Scheme 20). &sigma;*-Orbital interaction in&shy;volving the C<sub>2</sub>&ndash;O bond, there&shy;fore, is not essential to the stereoselectivity of the reac&shy;tions of the radi&shy;cals <strong>37</strong> and <strong>38</strong>. The reactions of these radicals, however, point to the critical factor in deter&shy;mining reaction stereoselectivity, namely, maintain&shy;ing effective inter&shy;action in the transition state between the singly occupied, <em>p</em>-type orbital on C-1 and the <em>p</em>-type orbital on the ring oxygen atom.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="f4.png" class="default internal" style="width: 290px; height: 213px;" width="290px" height="213px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44066/f4.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=290&amp;height=213" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">Study of the reactions of radicals with restricted conformations gen&shy;er&shy;ates a powerful argu&shy;ment in favor of the control that conformational and stereo&shy;electronic effects have on the reactivity of pyranos-1-yl radicals. Among the radicals of this type discussed thus far, there is little indication that steric effects have other than a minor role in determining stereo&shy;selec&shy;tivity. Study of com&shy;pounds that have particularly well shielded radical centers, however, shows that steric effects can be significant in the stereo&shy;selectivity of some pyranos-1-yl radical reactions.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_12"><span id="(6)._Steric_Effects_Revisited"></span><h5 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">(6). Steric Effects Revisited</h5>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">(a). Pyranos-1-yl Radicals</p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">Even though conformational and stereoelectronic effects usually have the dominant role in de&shy;ter&shy;mining stereoselectivity in the reactions of pyran&shy;os-1-yl radicals, comparing the reactions shown in equations 6 and 7 shows that steric effects can assert themselves when a sufficiently effec&shy;tive shielding group is present in a reacting molecule. Reaction of the glycosyl chloride <strong>52</strong> with allyltri-<em>n</em>-butyltin follows the now familiar pattern of &alpha;-face reaction that maintains trans&shy;ition-state inter&shy;action between orbitals on C-1 and the ring oxygen atom (eq 6).<sup>37</sup> This reac&shy;tion stands in contrast to that shown in eq 7 where the sterically demanding phthalimido group at C-2 causes a reversal of stereoselectivity in <em>C</em>-glycoside for&shy;ma&shy;tion. This second reaction (eq 7) is a striking example of steric effects over&shy;whelm&shy;ing the conformational and stereoelectronic effects that normally control stereo&shy;selectivity in reactions of pyranos-1-yl radicals.<sup>37,38</sup></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="(6).png" class="default internal" style="width: 435px; height: 132px;" width="435px" height="132px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44067/(6).png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=435&amp;height=132" /></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="(7).png" class="default internal" style="width: 440px; height: 167px;" width="440px" height="167px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44068/(7).png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=440&amp;height=167" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">Reaction of the bromide <strong>53</strong> with <em>tert</em>-butyl isocyanide gives additional insight into the competition between steric and stereoelectronic effects in deter&shy;mining the stereoselectivity of reaction of pyranos-1-yl radicals (Scheme 22).<sup>35</sup> The C-1 configuration in products <strong>55</strong> and <strong>56</strong> is determined by the stereo&shy;selectivity of the reaction between the radical <strong>54</strong> and <em>tert</em>-butyl iso&shy;cyanide. In this reaction steric effects, which favor &beta;-face reaction of <strong>54</strong>, are more power&shy;ful than the stereo&shy;elec&shy;tronic effects, which favor forming the product with an &alpha; configuration. In addition to shi&shy;elding due to the methylene carbon atom attached to C-2, the shape of the radical in the vicinity of the ethoxy group has major impact on the steric effects directing the approach of <em>tert</em>-butyl iso&shy;cyanide (Scheme 22).</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><br />
<img alt="s22.png" class="default internal" style="width: 385px; height: 652px;" width="385px" height="652px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44069/s22.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=385&amp;height=652" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">(b). Furanos-1-yl Radicals</p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">Study of pyranos-1&#8209;yl radicals has identified conformational mobility as a &ldquo;key&rdquo; factor in the stereo&shy;selectivity of their reactions. This selec&shy;tivity depends not only on which conformation is the major one but also on the accessibility and reactivity of other conformations. For furanos-1-yl radi&shy;cals the same factors are involved, but their relative importance may differ. Con&shy;for&shy;mational mobil&shy;ity is greater for furanos-1-yl radicals than for their pyran&shy;os-1-yl counterparts. Also, due to the shape of the furanoid ring, maintaining effec&shy;tive, transition-state interaction between <em>p</em>-type orbit&shy;als on C-1 and the ring oxygen atom (i.e., the interaction necessary for kinetic-anomeric sta&shy;bil&shy;i&shy;za&shy;tion) in many conformations is possible during reaction from both &alpha; and &beta; faces of the ring. These observations lead to the proposal that confor&shy;ma&shy;tional and stereo&shy;elec&shy;tronic effects may be less important in determining stereo&shy;selec&shy;tivity in furanos-1-yl radical reaction than in reactions of pyranos-1-yl radi&shy;cals. The available information on stereoselectivity of the reactions of furan&shy;os-1-yl radicals is not sufficient to draw a firm conclusion about the relative importance of the kinetic anomeric effect when compared to steric effects. Exclusive hydrogen-atom transfer to the less-hindered &beta; face of the radical <strong>57</strong> is consistent with steric effects controlling stereoselectivity; in addition, the fact that the radical <strong>58</strong>, for which the &beta; face is more hindered, is less stereoselective in its reaction also is consistent with steric effects primarily determining reaction stereo&shy;selectivity (Scheme 23).<sup>39</sup></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><br />
<img alt="s23.png" class="default internal" style="width: 350px; height: 493px;" width="350px" height="493px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44070/s23.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=350&amp;height=493" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">(c). Supersteric Radicals</p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">Although bonding between two radicals normally is a rare event, such a reaction can become sig&shy;nificant when other processes (e.g., hydrogen-atom abstraction or addition to an unsaturated com&shy;pound) take place too slowly. Bonding between two radicals begins at a sufficiently long distance that dif&shy;fer&shy;ences in transition-state energies leading to different products sometimes are too small to be of consequence; as a result, little or no stereo&shy;selec&shy;tivity is observed (eq 8).<sup>40</sup></p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="(8).png" class="default internal" style="width: 420px; height: 147px;" width="420px" height="147px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44071/(8).png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=420&amp;height=147" /></p>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">If one of the radicals involved in a coupling process has steric require&shy;ments that are great enough, even radical coupling becomes stereo&shy;selective. The &quot;supersteric&quot; Co(dmgH)<sub>2</sub>py radical, for example, has such a large steric requirement that its reaction with the pyranos-1-yl radical <strong>59</strong> occurs much more rapidly from the &beta; face of the pyranoid ring in <strong>59 </strong>than from its &alpha; face (Scheme 24).<sup>41 </sup>Having the Co(dmgH)<sub>2</sub>py substituent in an equatorial position (&beta; anomer) is so much more stable than having it in the more crowded axial orientation (&alpha; anomer) that this difference sig&shy;nif&shy;i&shy;cantly affects the transition-state energies leading to these two anomers. The steric size of the Co(dmgH)<sub>2</sub>py substituent is so great that it overcomes the usually more impor&shy;tant kinetic ano&shy;meric effect in determining reaction stereoselectivity.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s24.png" class="default internal" style="width: 415px; height: 437px;" width="415px" height="437px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44072/s24.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=415&amp;height=437" /></p>

</div></div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_(Binkley)/Radical_Reactions_of_Carbohydrates_I:_Structure_and_Reactivity_of_Carbohydrate_Radicals/11:_Stereoselectivity/III._Maximizing_Transition-State_Stabilization_by_Orbital_Interactions:_The_Kinetic_Anomeric_Effect" class="mt-section" id="section_13"><span id="2._Effect_of_Temperature_on_Stereoselectivity"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-23959 editable">2. Effect of Temperature on Stereoselectivity</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-23959">A final point with respect to stereoselectivity in bimolecular radical reactions con&shy;cerns re&shy;ac&shy;tion temperature. In the process shown in Scheme 25 stereo&shy;se&shy;lec&shy;tivity is determined by the approach of Bu<sub>3</sub>SnD to the intermediate pyranos-1-yl radical <strong>26</strong>.<sup>31</sup> According to the kinetic ano&shy;meric effect [Section III.B.1.c.(4).] stereoselective deuterium transfer to the &alpha; face of <strong>26</strong> (to give <strong>30</strong>) should have a lower transition-state energy than transfer to its &beta; face (to give <strong>31</strong>). At low tem&shy;perature the stereoselectivity is high, but as the temper&shy;ature rises, stereoselectivity decreases because a progressively larger percentage of radicals are able to react with Bu<sub>3</sub>SnD and transcend the barriers leading to both products (<strong>30</strong> and <strong>31</strong>). The results shown in Scheme 25 can be viewed as a general response of reaction stereoselectivity to changes in temperature.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="s25.png" class="default internal" style="width: 465px; height: 320px;" width="465px" height="320px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/44073/s25.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=465&amp;height=320" /></p>


